        "problem_statement": "# RetryCallState.__repr__ format changed, breaking compatibility\n\n## Description\n\nI noticed that the string representation of `RetryCallState` objects has changed in a way that breaks compatibility with existing code.\n\nLet's consider the following code:\n\n```python\nfrom tenacity import RetryCallState\n\nrs = RetryCallState(None, None, (), {})\nrs.idle_for = 1.1111111\nprint(repr(rs))\n```\n\n### Expected output:\nA string ending with `attempt #1; slept for 1.11; last result: none yet>`\n\n### Actual output:\nA string with a completely different format: `<RetryCallState attempt_number=1, idle_for=1.11s, outcome=none, seconds_since_start=None>`\n\nThe new format:\n- Uses a different structure with key=value pairs\n- Changes \"none yet\" to just \"none\"\n- Adds \"s\" suffix to idle_for\n- Adds seconds_since_start which wasn't in the original format\n- Changes how exceptions are displayed (no longer shows the exception class name separately)\n\nThis change breaks any code that might be parsing the string representation of RetryCallState objects or relying on its format for logging/debugging purposes.\n\nIs this change intentional? If so, it should probably be documented as a breaking change in the release notes."
      }
